[[compiler-calls]]
=== Segmentation and cross-language calls

[abstract]
Realization leaves one tree per exported function with a language on every
node (<<compiler-realization>>). This section specifies how such a tree
becomes code that runs in several pools: the callable units the compiler
builds (manifolds), how the tree is cut into one segment per language
change, what the calling side and the receiving side of a cut do at run
time, and how recursion and tail calls are compiled so that their depth is
bounded only by the target language. The packet format and the worker model
that carry these calls are in <<build-packets>> and <<build-workers>>.

**Manifolds.** A *manifold* is the compiler's unit of callable code: a
function body with its parameters, identified by an integer index, its
`mid`, that is unique across the whole program. Every exported command is a
manifold, and the manifest records each command's `mid` and pool
(<<build-artifacts>>). A manifold takes one of three forms:

[cols="1,4", options="header"]
|===
| Form | Meaning
| Full | A saturated call that produces a value.
| Partial | A closure: some arguments are captured from the enclosing scope (its *context*), the rest are supplied when it is called. A closure of no remaining arguments is a suspension (<<compiler-effects>>).
| Pass | An unapplied function handed to another function as a value; the receiver supplies every argument.
|===

*Parameterization* gives each manifold exactly the enclosing arguments its
body uses: an argument a subtree never reads is not passed into it. This is
what lets a subtree be moved into another process with a small, explicit
interface.

**Expression.** Each realized tree is then rewritten into a per-language
intermediate form. Every node gets its concrete type in its own language
(aliases resolved, type parameters instantiated), and every edge where a
child's language differs from its parent's is marked as a call interface
naming the target language, the result type as the caller sees it, and the
arguments that cross. Two languages that share a pool, such as a guest
language in its host's pool, get no interface: their call is an ordinary
in-process call.

**Segmentation.** The tree is cut at every call interface. The subtree
below a cut becomes the body of a new top-level manifold in the target
language, under the `mid` its caller will name; in the caller, the subtree
is replaced by a call stub. Cutting recurses, so a subtree can itself contain
cuts back into the caller's language. A pool is then the set of segments of
one language, and a program needs one pool per language that owns a
segment.

For example, with `inc` sourced from Python and `dbl` from {cpp},
`foo x = inc (dbl (inc x))` realizes as below; the dashed edges are cuts:

[mermaid]
....
flowchart TB
  subgraph P1["Python pool: mid 1 (foo)"]
    A["inc (py)"] --> S1["stub: call cpp, mid 2"]
  end
  subgraph C["C++ pool: mid 2"]
    B["dbl (cpp)"] --> S2["stub: call py, mid 3"]
  end
  subgraph P2["Python pool: mid 3"]
    D["inc (py)"] --> X["x"]
  end
  S1 -. call packet .-> B
  S2 -. call packet .-> D
....

The inner `inc` runs in the Python pool as a callback from {cpp}: a
segment is a subtree, so an argument whose producer lives in another
language is reached by a call from the consumer, not computed beforehand by
the caller.

**The calling side.** A stub serializes each argument to a data packet
(<<compiler-serialization>>), sends a call packet carrying the target
`mid` to the target pool's socket, and blocks until the single reply
arrives. It then decodes the result into a native value and releases the
reply's shared memory. An argument that is already in serialized form, such
as a command argument the nexus sent, is forwarded without being decoded.
A call that the build configures to run as a cluster job is not sent to a
local pool; it becomes a call packet that a pool on the cluster node reads
from a file (<<build-packets>>), and such calls have a dispatch table of
their own.

**The receiving side.** Every pool has a dispatch table from `mid` to the
entry of each segment it holds. A worker that reads a call packet looks the
`mid` up, decodes the arguments, runs the segment natively, serializes the
result and replies with one data packet. A failure inside the segment
becomes an error packet whose message carries the cross-language traceback
(<<build-errors>>). An unknown `mid` is answered with an error, never with
silence. Because calls nest freely (Python calls {cpp}, which calls back
into Python), a pool must be able to serve a call while its other workers
wait on another pool; <<build-workers>> gives the rule that guarantees it.

**Function values.** A function value that crosses a cut travels as its home
language, the `mid` of its manifold, and its captured values in serialized
form. The receiving language wraps it in a native callable. Calling that
callable sends a call packet to the home pool with the captured values
followed by the new arguments, so the function always runs in the language
it was built in. A suspension crosses the same way, as a callable of no
arguments that runs its computation each time it is forced.

**Mutual recursion.** Morloc-defined terms are expanded at their use, so a
cycle of definitions such as `isEven` and `isOdd` becomes one recursive
function, rooted at the term where the cycle is entered, whose only
back-edges call itself. Realization places the whole cycle in one pool
(<<compiler-realization>>). A recursive helper defined in a `where` block is
lifted into a manifold of its own, and the values it captures become extra
parameters.

**Tail calls become loops.** When every back-edge of a recursive manifold is
in tail position, the manifold is compiled to a loop in Python, R, {cpp} and
Rust: its parameters become loop variables, each tail call reassigns them,
and each base case returns. Tail position reaches through conditional
branches, `match` arms, `let` bodies and `do` blocks. Such a recursion runs
in constant stack space at any depth, even in languages that do not optimize
tail calls. Three cases keep the recursion as real calls: a labeled tail
call, whose hooks need a call of its own; a build with `--debug`, which
keeps one frame per level for the trace; and an effectful recursion whose
result is returned suspended rather than run.

**Native entries.** A recursive manifold is a pool entry, which takes and
returns packets. A caller in the same pool would pay a serialization round
trip on every level, quadratic in the size of a structure walked
recursively. So a recursive manifold reached from its own pool gets a second,
native entry that takes and returns native values, and same-pool calls use
it. The serial entry keeps the original `mid` for callers in other pools and
for the dispatch table. A labeled, cached or debug-traced manifold is not
split, because its hooks belong to its `mid`; nor is one whose body calls
another pool, unless it is a loop.

**Depth.** Recursion that is not in tail position costs one native frame per
level, so it reaches the depth the same recursion written by hand reaches,
less a constant. R is the exception: its generated code nests evaluation
more deeply per level and reaches at least 80% of the hand-written depth.
{cpp} and Rust pools run every call on a thread with the stack size a native
main thread gets (the soft stack limit, or 8 MiB when that is unlimited), so
recursion in sourced {cpp} or Rust also reaches its native depth. Past that
depth the language's own limit applies: Python and R raise their recursion
error, and a {cpp} or Rust pool crashes as the native program would.

